The '''2011 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League]], the 40th season of Europe's secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the second season since it was renamed from UEFA Cup to [[UEFA Europa League]]. The match was played at the [[Aviva Stadium]] in [[Dublin]], [[Republic of Ireland|Ireland]], on 18 May 2011,<ref name="uefa-venue">{{cite web |title=2011 final: Dublin Arena |url=http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/stadium/index.html |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=4 June 2011 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110519222022/http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/stadium/index.html| archive-date= 19 May 2011 | url-status= dead}}</ref> between two Portuguese teams – [[FC Porto|Porto]] and [[S.C. Braga|Braga]] – for the first time in UEFA competition finals.<ref name="guardian">{{cite news |title=Porto and Braga to meet in Portuguese Europa League final in Dublin |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/may/05/porto-braga-villarreal-benfica-europa-league |work=The Guardian|publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=5 May 2011 |access-date=10 May 2011 }}</ref>

Porto won 1–0 and secured their second title in the competition, following a successful appearance in the [[2003 UEFA Cup Final]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Falcao heads Porto to Europa League glory |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c50f8680b62-7a96c1afdbf0-1000--falcao-heads-porto-to-europa-league-glory/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 May 2011 |access-date=18 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110604113712/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/matches/season%3D2011/round%3D2000133/match%3D2006268/postmatch/report/index.html |archive-date=4 June 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref> The man of the match was Colombian striker [[Radamel Falcao]], who scored the winning goal and further cemented his 2010–11 UEFA Europa League top scorer tally with a tournament record of 17 goals.<ref>{{cite news |title=Prolific Falcao leads Porto to glory |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/316536?cc=5739 |work=ESPNsoccernet |publisher=ESPN Internet Ventures |date=18 May 2011 |access-date=24 May 2011 |archive-date=22 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110522062235/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/316536?cc=5739 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="falcao">{{cite news|title=Seventeen-goal Falcao signs off with record |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c50f81b7942-fac67f6ec49d-1000--seventeen-goal-falcao-signs-off-with-record/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 May 2011 |access-date=18 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110519215549/http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid%3D1630637.html |archive-date=19 May 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref>

As the winners, Porto earned the right to play against the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League]] winners, [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], in the [[2011 UEFA Super Cup]].

==Background==
The qualification of [[FC Porto|Porto]] and [[S.C. Braga|Braga]] for the 2011 UEFA Europa League Final ensured it would be the first all-[[Portuguese Football Federation|Portuguese]] final in [[UEFA]] competitions,<ref name="all-PT final">{{cite news |title=Portugal to star in eighth one-nation final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0253-0d7d151bf081-a8e1ab722655-1000--portugal-to-star-in-eighth-one-nation-final/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=5 May 2011 |access-date=28 May 2011 }}</ref> and only the second European tie between Portuguese teams, after the semi-final meeting between Braga and [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]].<ref name="pre-match stats">{{cite news |title=Porto and Braga take local rivalry to grand stage |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c50f6cb1632-2ae2abb78d43-1000--porto-and-braga-take-local-rivalry-to-grand-stage/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=15 May 2011 |access-date=28 May 2011 }}</ref> Overall, it was the eighth UEFA Cup or UEFA Europa League final featuring two clubs from the same national association.<ref name="all-PT final"/> A distance of {{convert|47.4|km|mi|abbr=on}} separated the cities of [[Porto]] and [[Braga]], the smallest between opponents in a UEFA competition final.<ref name="closest final">{{cite news |title=Braga and Porto the closest of final rivals |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0253-0d7d151ba59d-6502cf6794ae-1000--braga-and-porto-the-closest-of-final-rivals/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=6 May 2011 |access-date=28 May 2011 }}</ref> The previous record was {{convert|83.8|km|mi|abbr=on}}, set at the [[1988 European Super Cup]] between [[PSV Eindhoven]] of the [[Netherlands]] and [[KV Mechelen]] of [[Belgium]].<ref name="closest final"/>

Porto secured its presence in a major UEFA competition final for the fifth time, after victorious campaigns at the [[1986–87 European Cup]] (2–1 against [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]]), [[2002–03 UEFA Cup]] (3–2 against [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]) and [[2003–04 UEFA Champions League]] (3–0 against [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]]), and a runner-up place in the [[1984 European Cup Winners' Cup final]] (lost 2–1 against [[Juventus FC|Juventus]]). The club also competed three times in the [[UEFA Super Cup]] – won in [[1987 European Super Cup|1987]] (2–0 in aggregate, against [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]]) and lost in [[2003 UEFA Super Cup|2003]] (1–0 against [[AC Milan|Milan]]) and [[2004 UEFA Super Cup|2004]] (2–1 against [[Valencia CF|Valencia]]) – and grabbed two [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]] titles in [[1987 Intercontinental Cup|1987]] (1–0 against [[C.A. Peñarol|Peñarol]] of Uruguay) and [[2004 Intercontinental Cup|2004]] (0–0, 8–7 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]], against [[Once Caldas]] of Colombia).<ref name="pre-match stats"/>

Braga qualified for a UEFA competition final for the first time.<ref name="guardian"/> Before reaching the Dublin final, the club's best European result was a place in the last 16 of the [[2006–07 UEFA Cup|2006–07]] and [[2008–09 UEFA Cup]] seasons.<ref name="pre-match stats"/> Participation in the 2008–09 UEFA Cup was achieved via the [[2008 UEFA Intertoto Cup]], where Braga was one of the eleven third-round winners. Having reached the furthest in the UEFA Cup, among the [[UEFA Intertoto Cup|Intertoto Cup]] teams, Braga were considered the outright winners of the final season of this competition.<ref>{{cite web|title=UIC Winners 1995 - 2008 |url=http://www.el-sports.org/uic-winners |work=EL Sports.org |publisher=European Lotteries Sports |access-date=29 May 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120402115900/http://www.el-sports.org/uic-winners |archive-date=2 April 2012 }}</ref> In contrast to the extended national and international curriculum of Porto, Braga had just one major title in their history: the 1966 Portuguese Cup.<ref name="pre-match stats"/>

A [[UEFA Champions League]] regular, Porto finished third in the [[2009–10 Primeira Liga]] and thus missed a place in the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League]], entering instead in the UEFA Europa League play-off round.<ref>{{cite news |title=Big names await Europa League fate |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01e8-0e750e3cfd20-69831a149a9c-1000--big-names-await-europa-league-fate/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=6 August 2010 |access-date=29 May 2011 }}</ref> This was the first time Porto competed in UEFA's second competition since winning the 2002–03 tournament. Braga, on the other hand, finished the Portuguese league in a historic second place, which granted them participation in the UEFA Champions League for the first time.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cardozo seals title for Benfica |url=http://en.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=por/news/newsid=1486467.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100521050720/http://en.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=por/news/newsid=1486467.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 May 2010 |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 May 2010 |access-date=29 May 2011 }}</ref> Braga reached the group stage by successively knocking out high-profile opponents, such as Celtic and [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], in the second and third qualifying rounds, respectively.<ref>{{cite news |first=Reda |last=Maher |title=Champions League - Celtic out despite win |url=http://eurosport.yahoo.com/06082010/58/champions-league-celtic-despite-win.html |publisher=[[Eurosport]] |date=4 August 2010 |access-date=29 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120731203733/http://eurosport.yahoo.com/06082010/58/champions-league-celtic-despite-win.html |archive-date=31 July 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Lima hat-trick ensures history for Braga |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e8-0e753a36fec3-68601b00eab6-1000--lima-hat-trick-ensures-history-for-braga/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 August 2010 |access-date=29 May 2011 }}</ref> The club concluded the group stage in third place, behind [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] and [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], and was relegated into the UEFA Europa League round of 32.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shakhtar end their wait and Braga's bid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01ec-0e76938313b8-36be9c36cea3-1000--shakhtar-end-their-wait-and-braga-s-bid/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=8 December 2010 |access-date=29 May 2011 }}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:7o7Pw.jpg|thumb|[[Aviva Stadium]] was renamed to "Dublin Arena" for the match]]
The [[Aviva Stadium]] in Dublin, Ireland, was selected to host the final match of the 2011 UEFA Europa League, at a meeting of the UEFA Executive Committee, on 29 January 2009.<ref name="venue-uefa">{{cite news |title=Venues for 2011 and 2012 UEFA club finals designated |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=941911.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121102113459/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=941911.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2 November 2012 |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=29 January 2009 |access-date=31 May 2011 }}</ref><ref name="venue-bbc">{{cite news |title=Wembley to host 2011 Euro final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/7858932.stm |work=[[BBC Sport]] |publisher=[[BBC|British Broadcasting Corporation]] |date=29 January 2009 |access-date=31 May 2011 }}</ref> A bid from the then under-construction Irish venue was put forward by the [[Football Association of Ireland]] (FAI) and the [[Dublin City Council]].<ref name="venue-bbc2">{{cite news |title=Lansdowne Road to host Uefa final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/irish/7859156.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=29 January 2009 |access-date=31 May 2011 }}</ref> It had competition from [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]'s [[Emirates Stadium]], but as [[Wembley Stadium]] was picked to host the Champions League final, Arsenal's bid was ruled out on the grounds that UEFA does not allow the same country to host its two major competition finals in the same season.<ref name="venue-bbc"/> This selection confirmed that Ireland would host its first-ever European football club competition final.<ref name="venue-uefa2">{{cite news |title=Irish first looms for Lansdowne Road |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0254-0d7c87d3ed0b-7d8887c875e6-1000--irish-first-looms-for-lansdowne-road/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 January 2009 |access-date=31 May 2011 }}</ref>

The new 50,000 all-seater stadium was built on the site of the old [[Lansdowne Road]] ground, with the intention of continuing the location as the home of the Irish national [[Ireland national rugby union team|rugby]] and [[Republic of Ireland national football team|football]] teams.<ref name="venue-uefa2"/> Demolition work began in late May 2007 and lasted two months. Erection of the infrastructure initiated in January 2009, and by October, the roof was fully installed; in the following month, construction of the pitch took place. The stadium was completed and handed over in April 2010 to the FAI and [[Irish Rugby Football Union]] (IRFU).<ref name="venue-construction">{{cite web |title=Stadium information |url=http://www.avivastadium.ie/stadiumInformation.aspx |work=AvivaStadium.ie |publisher=New Stadium |access-date=1 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110721123201/http://www.avivastadium.ie/stadiumInformation.aspx |archive-date=21 July 2011 |url-status=dead}}</ref> It was officially opened on 14 May 2010,<ref name="venue-opening">{{cite web |title=Dublin Arena open for business |url=http://www.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=irl/news/newsid=1488379.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100516143717/http://www.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=irl/news/newsid=1488379.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=16 May 2010 |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=14 May 2010 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref> and on 11 August 2010 hosted its first international football match, between the Republic of Ireland and Argentina.<ref>{{cite news |title=Republic of Ireland 0-1 Argentina |url=http://www.rte.ie/sport/soccer/2010/0811/argentina.html |work=[[RTÉ Sport]] |publisher=[[Raidió Teilifís Éireann]] |date=11 August 2010 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref>

On 12 February 2009, the naming rights for the new stadium were bought by the British multi-national insurance company [[Aviva]], for a ten-year period.<ref name="venue-naming">{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Gallagher |title=Lansdowne to be renamed Aviva Stadium |url=http://www.irishtimes.com/sports/rugby/2009/0212/1224241050544.html |work=The Irish Times |date=12 February 2009 |access-date=1 June 2011 |archive-date=8 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121008125420/http://www.irishtimes.com/sports/rugby/2009/0212/1224241050544.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> However, because UEFA sponsorship regulations forbid advertising of brands outside of the organisation during competition matches,<ref name="rules">{{cite web |title=Regulations of the UEFA Europa League 2010/11 |url=http://en.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/Regulations/competitions/Regulations/01/48/48/90/1484890_DOWNLOAD.pdf|work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |pages=38–39 |access-date=1 June 2011 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110519232231/http://en.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/Regulations/competitions/Regulations/01/48/48/90/1484890_DOWNLOAD.pdf| archive-date= 19 May 2011 | url-status= dead}}</ref> the venue would be referred as "Dublin Arena" during the final.<ref name="dublin-arena">{{cite news |first=Ronan |last=McGreevy |title=Aviva rebranded for Europa final |url=http://www.irishtimes.com/newspaper/breaking/2011/0517/breaking50.html |work=The Irish Times|date=17 May 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref> Venue preparations for the final included the removal of every advertising and stadium branding elements unrelated with UEFA and its official sponsor partners. Stadium director Martin Murphy explained that the whole process was a "big operation", through which they had to "cover the signs on the stadium facade itself" and that "the three tiers [would] have Uefa {{sic}} branding across them, covering any existing sponsors." It also involved an increase in the number of media facilities and seats.<ref>{{cite news |first=Lisa |last=Jackson |title=Final countdown fever involves covering Aviva |url=http://www.sbpost.ie/news/ireland/final-countdown-fever-involves-covering-aviva-56188.html |work=The Post.ie |publisher=Post Publications |location=Cork |date=8 May 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref>

