==Pre-match==

===Identity===
UEFA unveiled the visual identity of the 2011 UEFA Europa League final in a ceremony held on 30 November 2010 at Dublin's Convention Centre. Attending this event were delegates of the local civic authorities, the Irish government and the FAI.<ref name="branding">{{cite news |first=Aidan |last=Fitzmaurice |title=Dublin takes pride in visual identity for final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01eb-0ea1b8a6b45c-f43e0bf792a1-1000--dublin-takes-pride-in-visual-identity-for-final/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 November 2010 |access-date=30 May 2011 }}</ref><ref name="fai">{{cite news |title=UEFA Europa League Final 2011 and its event design launched in Dublin |url=http://www.fai.ie/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=101438:uefa-europa-league-final-2011-and-its-event-design-launched-in-dublin&catid=75:about-fai&Itemid=139 |work=FAI.ie |publisher=[[Football Association of Ireland]] |date=30 November 2010 |access-date=30 May 2011 }}</ref> London-based brand agency Desigwerk was again awarded the task of conceiving the visual identity for a UEFA competition event, after the [[2005 UEFA Champions League Final|2005]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League Final|2007 UEFA Champions League finals]] and the [[UEFA Euro 2008]].<ref>{{cite news |title=UEFA Europa League Final Dublin 2011 |url=http://www.designwerk.co.uk/latest_news.html |publisher=Designwerk |date=30 November 2011 |access-date=30 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110515013952/http://www.designwerk.co.uk/latest_news.html |archive-date=15 May 2011 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The Dublin Arena was depicted as the focal element of the logo design, as the stadium is "the ultimate destination for the fans on their journey", according to UEFA's head of club competitions, Michael Heselschwerdt. He further explained that the visual identity contained "very clear and specific mentions and visual references to Dublin, to the Celtic setting and history, and to the local visual iconography".<ref name="branding"/>

===Ticketing===
Due to security reasons, the 50,000-seat Dublin Arena had its capacity reduced to 47,000 for the UEFA Europa League final.<ref name="venue-construction"/><ref name="examiner">{{cite news |first=Liam |last=Mackey |title=Delaney: Europa final will be sellout |url=http://www.irishexaminer.com/sport/kfojkfsnqlkf/ |newspaper=[[Irish Examiner]] |publisher=Examiner Publications |location=Cork |date=20 April 2011 |access-date=31 May 2011 }}</ref> Ticket sales for the general public were carried out in two phases. The first phase was open exclusively for Irish residents from 16 December 2010 to 13 January 2011.<ref name="tickets1">{{cite news |title=Dublin final tickets go on sale |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0254-0d7ca2ccdef9-ca0e174ecf53-1000--dublin-final-tickets-go-on-sale/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 December 2010 |access-date=30 May 2011 }}</ref> Applications were submitted online to UEFA and the FAI for a maximum of two tickets from one of the four available price categories.<ref name="tickets1"/> Validated requests were entered into a ballot to determine which applications would be allocated tickets; successful applicants were notified by 26 January and received the tickets by 25 April.<ref name="tickets2">{{cite news |title=Final ticket sales close for Irish residents |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01ed-0ea1e220aff9-b30c4c8b1f74-1000--final-ticket-sales-close-for-irish-residents/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=13 January 2011 |access-date=30 May 2011 }}</ref> FAI chief executive John Delaney revealed that 7,000 tickets were sold during this phase, from a total of 21,000 applications, and that an additional 3,000 tickets were already allocated to the FAI grassroots.<ref name="examiner"/> On 21 February, a second phase was opened for the international public, and lasted until 21 March. First-phase procedures were also applied to the international ticket sales. Ticket ballot notifications were concluded by 31 March and tickets were delivered by 18 April.<ref name="tickets3">{{cite news |title=UEFA Europa League final ticket sales over |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01ef-0ea22a1815f6-5efbfd53727e-1000--uefa-europa-league-final-ticket-sales-over/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=21 March 2011 |access-date=30 May 2011 }}</ref>

Each finalist club was assigned 12,000 tickets, most of which from the lowest priced category.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liga Europa: finalistas recebem 12 mil bilhetes cada |url=http://www.maisfutebol.iol.pt/taca-uefa/bilhetes-fc-porto-sp-braga-liga-europa-final-dublin/1251413-1499.html |work=Maisfutebol |publisher=Media Capital |language=pt |date=6 May 2011 |access-date=31 May 2011 |archive-date=9 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110509181655/http://www.maisfutebol.iol.pt/taca-uefa/bilhetes-fc-porto-sp-braga-liga-europa-final-dublin/1251413-1499.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> Porto requested their full share, which they sold to annual seat-owner club members, during an initial phase, and then to the remaining club membership.<ref>{{cite news |title=Venda de bilhetes para Dublin alargada a sócios |url=https://desporto.sapo.pt/futebol/liga_europa/artigo/2011/05/12/venda_de_bilhetes_para_dublin_al.html |work=Sapo Desporto |publisher=SAPO |language=pt |date=12 May 2011 |access-date=31 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110515194039/http://desporto.sapo.pt/futebol/liga_europa/artigo/2011/05/12/venda_de_bilhetes_para_dublin_al.html |archive-date=15 May 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Braga did not make complete use of their portion, returning a part of their allotted tickets.<ref>{{cite news |first=Derek |last=Reid |title=FAI look to boost attendances |url=http://www.sportsnewsireland.com/soccer_irish/23975/ |publisher=SportsNews Ireland |date=10 May 2011 |access-date=31 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120316174736/http://www.sportsnewsireland.com/soccer_irish/23975/ |archive-date=16 March 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Ambassador===
[[File:Ronnie Whelan.JPG|thumb|[[Ronnie Whelan]], 2011 UEFA Europa League Final ambassador]] 
Former Republic of Ireland and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] midfielder [[Ronnie Whelan]] was presented by UEFA as the ambassador for the Dublin 2011 UEFA Europa League final, during the draw for the competition's group stage, held on 27 August 2010 at the [[Grimaldi Forum]] in [[Monaco]].<ref name="whelan">{{cite news |title=Whelan flying the flag for Dublin |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c50f4948c6b-6de524305204-1000--whelan-flying-the-flag-for-dublin/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 August 2010 |access-date=29 May 2011 }}</ref> Arriving at the [[Merseyside]] club in 1979, Whelan became an important piece of the 1980s Liverpool team, winning six English titles, two [[FA Cup]]s and three [[Football League Cup|League Cups]], as well as the [[1984 European Cup Final|1984 European Cup]].<ref name="whelan2">{{cite web |title=The Ambassador: Ronnie Whelan |url=http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/ambassador/index.html |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=29 May 2011 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110519211641/http://en.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/final/ambassador/index.html| archive-date= 19 May 2011 | url-status= dead}}</ref> He helped the Republic of Ireland reach the [[1988 UEFA European Football Championship]], its first major international tournament; he was also in the squads that qualified Ireland to the [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]] and [[1994 FIFA World Cup]]s.<ref name="whelan2"/>

===Related events===
The [[UEFA Cup (trophy)|UEFA Europa League trophy]] was handed over by UEFA to the city of Dublin, in a ceremony held on 19 April 2011, at the [[Royal Hospital Kilmainham]]. During the event that marked the one-month countdown to the 2011 UEFA Europa League final, UEFA president [[Michel Platini]] entrusted the cup to [[Gerry Breen]], [[Lord Mayor of Dublin]], so that it would go into public display throughout the Irish capital until the day of the final. Representatives from [[Atlético Madrid]], the first Europa League winners – namely president [[Enrique Cerezo]], managing director Clemente Villaverde, and defender [[Álvaro Domínguez Soto|Álvaro Dominguez]]. FAI chief executive John Delaney, and the final ambassador Ronnie Whelan were also in attendance.<ref name="trophy-uefa1">{{cite news |first=Aidan |last=Fitzmaurice |title=Dublin receives UEFA Europa League trophy |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01f0-0ea240997af5-3a8d8862cfd6-1000--dublin-receives-uefa-europa-league-trophy/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=19 April 2011 |access-date=2 June 2011 }}</ref> Upon accepting the trophy, Breen stated that "Irish football fans have travelled the world supporting the Republic of Ireland but this is the first time that an event like this has come to our shores and this will be a major occasion for the city".

A trophy tour was set up so that the general public could see closely the award in various sites of Dublin, such as the [[General Post Office (Ireland)|General Post Office]], the [[Dublin City Hall]], and the recently built [[Dublin Airport#Terminal 2|airport terminal 2]].<ref name="trophy-fai">{{cite news|title=Grab your chance to see the Europa League Trophy |url=http://www.fai.ie/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=101720:grab-your-chance-to-see-the-europa-league-trophy&catid=76:aviva-stadium&Itemid=143 |work=FAI.ie |publisher=Football Association of Ireland |date=27 April 2011 |access-date=2 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110524215052/http://www.fai.ie/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=101720%3Agrab-your-chance-to-see-the-europa-league-trophy&catid=76%3Aaviva-stadium&Itemid=143 |archive-date=24 May 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Officials===
The refereeing team for the 2011 UEFA Europa League final was provided by the [[Royal Spanish Football Federation]] and led by referee [[Carlos Velasco Carballo]].<ref name="referee"/> Velasco Carballo began refereeing in the [[La Liga|Spanish Primera División]] in 2004, and in April 2005 he was selected as the fourth official for three matches in the elite round of the [[2005 UEFA European Under-19 Football Championship]]. In 2008, he was promoted to the [[FIFA]] list of international referees and refereed his first international matches in the [[2008 UEFA European Under-19 Football Championship elite qualification|elite qualification round of the 2008 UEFA European Under-19 Championship]]. He took charge of his first UEFA Champions League qualifying match later that year, followed by another in the 2009–10 season, as well as six UEFA Europa League matches. In 2010–11, he refereed his first matches in the UEFA Champions League proper, including three group stage games and the first leg of the semi-final between [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Palmarès for Carlos Velasco Carballo |url=http://www.worldreferee.com/site/copy.php?linkID=1074&linkType=referee&contextType=stats |publisher=WorldReferee.com |access-date=16 May 2011 |archive-date=16 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120316004725/http://www.worldreferee.com/site/copy.php?linkID=1074&linkType=referee&contextType=stats |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Velasco Carballo's refereeing team was all-Spanish: he was supported by assistant referees Roberto Alonso Fernández and Jesús Calvo Guadamuro on the touchlines, and [[Carlos Clos Gómez]] and Antonio Rubinos Pérez in the penalty areas, as well as fourth official David Fernández Borbolán and reserve assistant referee Juan Carlos Yuste Jiménez.<ref name="referee"/>

===Team selection===
Porto midfielder [[João Moutinho]] missed his team's final league match against [[C.S. Marítimo|Marítimo]], following an injury in the previous game against [[F.C. Paços de Ferreira|Paços de Ferreira]], but recovered in time to be included in [[André Villas-Boas]]'s picks for the Europa League final. Brazilian players [[Hulk (footballer)|Hulk]], [[Fernando (footballer, born 1987)|Fernando]], and goalkeeper [[Helton Arruda|Helton]] were also called by Villas-Boas after resting against Marítimo, in exchange for Polish keeper [[Paweł Kieszek]] and Argentine winger [[Mariano González]], who were not inscribed in the Europa League. Among the players unfit to play in Dublin due to more serious injuries or physical restraints were the Uruguayans [[Jorge Fucile]] (broken collarbone) and [[Cristian Rodríguez]], as well as Portuguese leftback [[Emídio Rafael]] (broken fibula).<ref name="lesions-porto">{{cite news |title=Moutinho convocado para a final da Liga Europa |url=http://desporto.publico.pt/noticia.aspx?id=1494373 |newspaper=[[Público (Portugal)|Público]] |agency=[[Lusa – Agência de Notícias de Portugal, SA|Lusa]] |language=pt |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=3 June 2011 }}</ref>

Braga's manager [[Domingos Paciência]] had no major physical impediments among his players. Despite pre-selected for the league match against [[Sporting CP]], defenders [[Miguel Garcia (Portuguese footballer)|Miguel Garcia]] and [[Alberto Junior Rodríguez|Alberto Rodríguez]], and striker [[Paulo César Rocha Rosa|Paulo César]] were deemed unfit to play and were left out by caution. They were reintegrated in the group that was to play in Dublin.<ref name="lesions-braga">{{cite news |title=Domingos chama todos os jogadores para final de Dublin |url=http://desporto.publico.pt/noticia.aspx?id=1494376 |newspaper=Público |agency=Lusa |language=pt |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=3 June 2011 }}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
Coming into this final once again as underdogs, Braga quickly showed signs of their intent as [[Custódio Castro|Custódio]] missed an early chance to goal with a wide shot from the right side of the penalty area. In response, in the 7th minute, [[Hulk (footballer)|Hulk]] jinked three Braga defenders and sent the ball inches away from the top-left corner.<ref name="summary-goal">{{cite news |first=Vasil |last=Kotsev |title=Porto 1-0 Braga: Radamel Falcao heads Dragons to Europa League glory |url=http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/59540/porto-vs-braga/report |publisher=Goal.com |date=18 May 2011 |access-date=4 June 2011 |archive-date=14 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180914203247/http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/59540/porto-vs-braga/report |url-status=dead }}</ref> Soon after, Braga transitioned into a more defensive strategy, holding much of Porto's attacks. The rest of the first half was characterised by few opportunities and excessive tackling, until the 44th minute, when Porto broke the deadlock.<ref name="summary-bbc">{{cite news |title=Europa League final: Porto 1-0 Braga |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13430716 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=18 May 2011 |access-date=4 June 2011 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110520052319/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/13430716.stm| archive-date= 20 May 2011 | url-status= live}}</ref> A loose pass from [[Alberto Junior Rodríguez|Alberto Rodriguez]] allowed [[Fredy Guarín]] to recover the ball on the midfield and execute a perfect deep cross for unmarked [[Radamel Falcao]] to conclude with a header into the top left corner, out of reach for Braga's keeper [[Artur Moraes]].<ref name="summary-uefa">{{cite web |title=UEFA Europa League - Porto-Braga: Commentary |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2006268--porto-vs-braga/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 May 2011 |access-date=4 June 2011 }}</ref> This was Falcao's 17th goal in the competition, which further cemented his already confirmed 2010–11 UEFA Europa League top scorer status, and contributed for his pick as man of the match.

Paciência introduced [[Kaká (footballer, born 1981)|Kaká]] and [[Márcio Mossoró]] into the game, right after half-time,<ref name="summary-uefa"/> and it took no time for the latter to create Braga's best opportunity to level the score. Stealing the ball from an unaware [[Rolando (footballer)|Rolando]], he raced towards [[Helton Arruda|Helton]] only to see his shot defended by the keeper's legs.<ref name="summary-bbc"/> Braga began pressing Porto and substitute [[Albert Meyong|Meyong]] had a chance for goal on the 77th minute, but his shot at the edge of the box ended in Helton's hands.<ref name="summary-goal"/> Porto continued holding off Braga's attempts and created more danger in counter-attacks, but the score would not change until the final whistle.<ref name="summary-bbc"/>

